JWT Authentication Bypass via Unverified Signature

PortSwigger Web Security Academy Lab

Introduction

Today I solved the lab JWT authentication bypass via unverified signature from PortSwigger Web Security Academy.

Before starting this lab, I first read about JSON Web Token (JWT), JWT signature, JWS, and common JWT attacks. After understanding these concepts, I started the lab.

At first, I was not getting it and tried many ways, but nothing worked. I got confused, so I stopped, went back again, and understood the JWT flow properly. After that, I came back to the lab and finally solved it.

This write-up is based completely on my own learning and hands-on practice.

Lab Goal

The goal of this lab is:

Login as a normal user

Exploit JWT authentication issue

Login as administrator

Delete the user carlos

Solve the lab

Starting the Lab

First, I logged in as wiener.

Then I opened Burp Suite and installed these extensions:

JWT Editor

JSON Web Tokens

These extensions helped me decode and modify JWT tokens easily.

Finding the JWT Token

After login, I intercepted the My Account request.

In the request cookies, I saw a session token, which was a JWT.

I decoded the JWT and saw the payload:

"username": "wiener"


This showed that the application was trusting the username from the JWT payload.

Modifying the JWT Payload

First, I changed:

"username": "wiener"


to:

"username": "carlos"


I sent the request and it worked.

Then I changed it again to:

"username": "administrator"


It worked again, which confirmed that the JWT signature was not being verified.

Getting Confused Again

I thought the lab was solved, but when I copied the admin URL, it redirected me back to the login page.

So I went back again and checked:

Admin requests

JWT token in Burp Repeater

JSON Web Tokens tab

Final Step to Solve the Lab

I intercepted the admin request and sent it to Burp Repeater.

Inside Repeater:

I modified the JWT payload to:

"username": "administrator"


Then I sent this request:

GET /admin/delete?username=carlos HTTP/2


The request was accepted, the user carlos was deleted, and the lab was solved.

Why This Vulnerability Is Dangerous

This vulnerability is dangerous because:

JWT payload can be modified easily

Signature is not verified

Attacker can become admin

Full application compromise is possible

What I Learned from This Lab

JWT signature verification is very important

Never trust JWT payload blindly

Understanding basics helps a lot

It is okay to get stuck and retry

Practice makes concepts clear

